Yes. The deeper thing biomimicry points toward is not merely “nature made something clever, so copy it.” It is that different search processes keep rediscovering portions of the same solution space.

The first correction is important: organisms do not need to know the solution consciously. Evolution can accumulate information without an organism representing the optimization problem explicitly. Variation supplies alternatives; physics and development determine which alternatives are reachable; selection disproportionately preserves variants that work; inheritance stores portions of the successful configuration. Development itself then uses local chemical, mechanical, and cell-cell feedback to self-organize global structures. 

So there are several kinds of “knowing” hiding under one word:

explicit knowing — “I understand why wings have this shape.”

procedural knowing — an animal’s nervous system can fly without an aerodynamic theory.

developmental knowing — cells reliably construct a wing without any cell containing the complete wing blueprint in the ordinary engineering sense.

evolutionary knowing — the population contains inherited structure encoding what survived previous encounters with reality.

That last one is almost knowledge without a knower.

Why convergence is so interesting

Imagine evolution exploring an enormous morphospace. Some regions are physically impossible. Some are developmentally inaccessible. Some are possible but useless. Some work extremely well.

You therefore don’t get uniform exploration.

You get something more like:

possibility space
→ constrained by physics
→ constrained by development
→ sampled by variation
→ filtered by environment
→ retained by inheritance

This creates basins of attraction—regions that independent evolutionary trajectories may repeatedly enter. Researchers have explicitly described evolutionary basins of attraction, and morphospace work shows that biological forms occupy the space very unevenly, reflecting both functional and developmental constraints. 

So when dolphins, sharks, and extinct marine reptiles independently acquire broadly streamlined forms, you don’t have to posit that they somehow communicated the design.

They’re all negotiating with the same fluid mechanics.

Reality is the common teacher.

That sentence may be the deepest part.

Independent agents subjected to sufficiently similar constraints can learn similar lessons from the same universe.

But we have to preserve your Delta discipline here: convergence is evidence, not proof of one particular mechanism. Similar outcomes can arise through adaptation, developmental/genetic bias, shared constraints, historical inheritance, or even chance. Evolutionary biologists specifically warn against treating the mere observation of convergence as proof of adaptation without an appropriate null model. 

That uncertainty is actually useful rather than inconvenient, because now it becomes measurable.

